A `toml` FENCE INCLUDES A PRIVATE, VENDORED GRAMMAR; AN `ini` FENCE INCLUDES VS CODE'S OWN. Both
close on their own backtick closer no matter what they hold open, so an unterminated construct
inside a closed fence stays opaque text to the host — the host's own fence recovery never sees
it. TOML 1.0 forms, TOML 1.1 forms, invalid escapes, a quoted-dotted table header, and an `ini`
fence come first; the specimens that leave something open INSIDE their own (still-closed) fence
come last.

```toml
title = "TOML example"
[owner]
name = "Tom"
dob = 1979-05-27T07:32:00Z

[database]
enabled = true
ports = [ 8001, 8001, 8002 ]
data = [ ["gamma", "delta"], [1, 2] ]
temp_targets = { cpu = 79.5, case = 72.0 }

[servers]

[servers.alpha]
ip = "10.0.0.1"
role = "frontend"
```

```toml
# TOML 1.1: optional seconds in a bare time and a space-separated datetime.
lt = 14:15
odt = 2010-02-03 14:15Z

# TOML 1.1: the \e and \xHH escapes, alongside the TOML 1.0 escape set.
esc = "bell\e hex\x61"
```

```toml
[fruit."var.ies"]
# The dot inside the quoted segment stays part of ONE key, not a table nesting.
name = "granny smith"
```

```ini
; An ini fence includes VS Code's own source.ini, not the toml grammar.
[section]
key = value
```

```toml
# An unterminated multi-line string inside an otherwise CLOSED fence: opaque to the host,
# and the fence's own closer still ends the block.
key = """an unterminated triple-quoted string
```

```toml
# An unclosed array, likewise opaque and likewise closed by the fence's own closer.
key = [1, 2,
```
